※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A record is its fields, and a value of one gives a value for each:
※ `Point(.x ← 3u32, .y ← 4u32)`.  It is written the way a call names a
※ parameter, which is the same idea asked of a field -- the mark says the name
※ is the thing's and not a variable's -- and it is the spelling C, Odin and Zig
※ give a structure's initializer.
※
※ **Every field is given and each once** (4577, 4578).  There is no default to
※ fall back on and nothing that would be right to leave behind: a field left out
※ would be storage holding whatever was there, which is the thing this language
※ does not have.  A name that is not a field is refused (4576) and a value given
※ without naming one is refused as well (4579), a record's fields having names
※ and a tuple being the shape for several values that go by position.
※
※ **A field is read with the same mark**: `p.x`.  It is the third thing that
※ mark does -- a module's name, an enumeration's value, and now a record's
※ field -- told apart by what stands on its left, which is what Go, Rust and Zig
※ all do.
※
※ **A record travels as its fields.**  To everything below the checker it is
※ what a tuple is: several values going together, placed by the convention the
※ same way, so a record handed to a call and answered with needs nothing of its
※ own.  What differs is that one of them named its parts.
※
※ **A record *name* stands for storage of its own**, which is what a name a
※ reference is taken of already does.  A field is read at an offset from it, so
※ `&p.x` is an address like any other and a field behind a reference is read
※ without reading the whole record first.  Binding a record to a name writes the
※ fields into that storage, which is what a record being a value rather than a
※ place means: two names are two records.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, **Go** and **Zig**, whose structure literals name the
※ fields and whose access is the same mark; **C**, whose designated initializer
※ this is written like and which also lets a field be left out, filling it with
※ zero -- which is the half not taken here.

type Point = x : u32 ; y : u32

fn total(p: Point) → u32:
    p.x + p.y

fn moved(p: Point, by: u32) → Point:
    Point(.x ← p.x + by, .y ← p.y + by)

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut u32):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1u32

fn seen(through: &Point) → u32:
    ※ read through a reference, without reading the whole record first
    through⌖.x + through⌖.y

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let p : Point = Point(.x ← 3u32, .y ← 4u32)
    ※ handed to a call, and answered with
    let q : Point = moved(p, 10u32)
    ※ a field read out of each
    let both : u32 = total(p) + total(q)
    ※ a reference to one field, and one to the whole record
    let counted : mut Point = Point(.x ← 0u32, .y ← 5u32)
    bump(&counted.x)
    let whole : &Point = &p
    if p.x ≠ 3u32 ∨ p.y ≠ 4u32:
        1u6
    elif q.x ≠ 13u32 ∨ q.y ≠ 14u32:
        2u6
    elif both ≠ 34u32:
        3u6
    elif counted.x ≠ 1u32:
        4u6
    elif seen(whole) ≠ 7u32 ∨ whole⌖.y ≠ 4u32:
        5u6
    else:
        0u6
